<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thermaic Gulf</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gulf of Thessaloniki</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Thermaic Gulf is an inlet of the north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pieria">Pieria</a>, Macedonia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcidice">Chalcidice</a> peninsula, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a> at its head. It takes its name from the ancient town of Therma, on whose site Thessaloniki was founded in 315 BCE. Rivers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardar">Axios</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haliacmon">Haliacmon</a> flow into it, and Greek colonies such as Methone and Pydna stood on its shores.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thermaic-gulf.jpg" alt="Thermaic Gulf" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eftychia Zerde · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coast_of_Perea,_Thessaloniki_prefecture,_Greece.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
